# kevinpierik.dev

## What this site is

`kevinpierik.dev` is a one-page professional profile and curriculum vitae. The canonical public document is `/`; unknown routes display the BIOS-style 404. There is no CMS. The homepage is a deliberately simple, hardcoded page: `app/(web)/page.tsx` owns the metadata, header, `<h1>`, grid, and structured data, while the CV content is written as markdown in `app/(web)/home.mdx`, without a content model or per-section components.

| Layer      | Choice                                                                                 |
| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Framework  | Next.js 16 App Router, static output for every route                                   |
| UI         | React Server Components; client components only where interaction is required (BIOS)   |
| Styling    | Tailwind CSS v4, CSS-first config in `features/style/`                                 |
| Content    | MDX via `@next/mdx`; element styling and layout components in `mdx-components.tsx`     |
| Fonts      | Helvetica Neue via `next/font/local` in `features/fonts/`                              |
| Tooling    | ESLint (`eslint-config-next`) for linting, Prettier for formatting, Vitest for tests   |
| Runtime    | Bun locally and on the host; deployed on Vercel                                        |
| Analytics  | `@vercel/analytics` and `@vercel/speed-insights`, only when `VERCEL_ENV` is set        |
| Behaviour  | UI SDK (`@ui-sdk/react`, pinned beta): headless primitives and hooks, styled with Tailwind |

Next.js 16 APIs and conventions may differ from what you know. Before writing Next.js-specific code, read the relevant guide in `node_modules/next/dist/docs/`, which always matches the installed version.

Agent skills live in `.agents/skills/`; `.claude/skills` is a symlink to it and `skills-lock.json` records their sources. Install or update them with `npx skills add <repo> --skill <name> --agent claude-code`, never edit them by hand, and keep them out of Prettier. The installed skills are `ui-sdk`, the Next.js workflow skills from `vercel/next.js`, `agent-browser`, and from `vercel-labs/agent-skills`: React best practices, composition patterns, view transitions, web design guidelines, writing guidelines, and Vercel optimize. Use `bunx agent-browser` (a dev dependency) to verify UI changes, such as the BIOS keyboard shortcuts, in a real browser. Before writing UI SDK code, read the `ui-sdk` skill and the docs in `node_modules/@ui-sdk/react/docs/`.

Run `bun run qa` before considering work complete. It checks formatting, lints, type-checks, runs the tests, and builds the production deployment. Run `bun run format` before committing.

## Folder layout

```text
app/          routes, route groups, metadata routes, and the shared HTML shell
components/   small, feature-agnostic building blocks (JSON-LD)
features/     BIOS 404, fonts, and styles
public/       static assets: icons and the Open Graph image
mdx-components.tsx   global MDX element styles and the Column, Section, Label, and Contact components
```

Keep the number of components small. Content is hardcoded at its point of use; only extract a component or feature when it carries real behaviour or is reused. Imports use `@/*`, which maps to the project root.

## Routes and metadata

- `app/(web)/page.tsx` is the canonical public page. It owns all of its metadata (title, description, canonical URL, language alternate, Open Graph, Twitter, robots) and renders the profile JSON-LD. Next.js merges `openGraph` shallowly, so a page that sets any Open Graph field must declare the complete object.
- `app/shared-web-layout.tsx` owns the `<html>`/`<body>` shell, the `lang` attribute, the font variable, global styles, and Vercel-only analytics. It also exports `sharedMetadata` (`metadataBase`, icons, format detection) and `sharedViewport` (light and dark color scheme and theme colour), which `app/(web)/layout.tsx` uses. The 404 declares its own dark-only viewport and passes `dark` to `SharedWebLayout`.
- `app/global-not-found.tsx` renders the BIOS 404. It must remain noindex. `BiosScreen` owns its own landmarks: a `<header>` for the top bar, a `<main id="main">` with the boot log as paragraphs and a route list (spacing comes from CSS, not from literal spaces, because Prettier collapses repeated spaces in JSX text) followed by the error as the page’s `<h1>`, and a `<footer>` with the reboot link and `<kbd>` key hints. Only Enter, Escape, and F1 trigger the reboot, registered with UI SDK’s `useShortcut` and filtered by `isPlainKeyPress` from `features/bios/reboot-keys.ts`, which also gives the reboot link its `aria-keyshortcuts`, so repeats and modified presses are ignored; never add global character-key or pointer handlers, because they break scrolling, text selection, and screen-reader keys (WCAG 2.1.4 and 2.5.2).
- `robots.txt`, `sitemap.xml`, and `llms.txt` must list or allow only canonical public content. API paths stay disallowed in `robots.txt`.
- Every route must stay static (`○` in the build output). Route handlers export `dynamic = "force-static"`.

## Accessibility and SEO

The homepage has one visible `<h1>` for the person’s name. Each top-level CV section has an `<h2>`; individual education, skill, and employment entries use `<h3>`. Do not create `h4`–`h6` merely to use every tag: add a deeper heading only for a genuine subordinate content section, and never skip heading ranks.

Use landmarks and accessible names for main content, navigation, and sections. Maintain visible focus styles, semantic lists for grouped information, native links for external destinations, and the reduced-motion rules in `features/style/animations.css`.

The CV content in `app/(web)/home.mdx` uses plain markdown: `###` for entries, `-` lists for their details, and markdown links. Wrap content in `<Column className="…">` for grid placement and `<Section title="…">`, which renders the `<section aria-labelledby>` landmark and its `<h2>` with an id slugified from the title. Wrap the Contact list in `<Contact>`, which renders `<address>`, and use `<Label title="Phone">P:</Label>` for its hanging labels: the visible abbreviation is hidden from assistive technology and `title` is read instead. Mark dates and durations with `<time dateTime="…">`, such as `<time dateTime="2023-09">September 2023</time>` and `<time dateTime="P5M">5 months</time>`. Styling for markdown elements lives only in `mdx-components.tsx`; do not put Tailwind classes in the MDX beyond `Column` and `Section` layout classes.

Structured data is hardcoded in `app/(web)/page.tsx` as a `Graph` typed with `schema-dts` and rendered through `components/json-ld.tsx`. Profile details are repeated in the page, its metadata, the structured data, `home.mdx`, and `llms.txt`; when you change one, change the others.

## Styling and performance

Global styling is imported only through `app/shared-web-layout.tsx`; tokens live under `features/style/`. The site follows the system color scheme without a toggle: `features/style/colors.css` defines `--background` and `--foreground` with `light-dark()`, exposed to Tailwind as `background` and `foreground`, so use those instead of hardcoded black or white. The `.dark` class forces the dark scheme and is used only by the BIOS 404. Text requires at least a 4.5:1 contrast ratio in both schemes. The `::selection` background is the foreground colour at 99% opacity rather than opaque, because WebKit blends opaque selection colours with white and would render them grey. The BIOS 404 keeps its original palette as arbitrary Tailwind classes in `features/bios/bios-screen.tsx`, a Server Component; its browser-only parts (the reboot shortcut and the requested route) live in `features/bios/bios-client.ts`. Server components are the default; add `"use client"` only where client-side interaction is required.

## Testing

Tests use Vitest and live next to the code they cover as `*.test.ts` or `*.test.tsx`. Only test real logic or behaviour that is easy to break, such as helpers and escaping; do not test hardcoded content or framework behaviour. Import test APIs explicitly from `vitest`. Run them with `bun run test`, or `bun run test:watch` during development.

## Conventions

- Write everything in English: commit messages, PR descriptions, file names, code symbols, UI copy, and repository documentation.
- Use kebab-case file names.
- Put local environment values in `.env.local`; document required variables in `.env.example`.
- Follow Zed’s commit style: `area: Description`, where `area` is the lowercase feature or folder (`bios`, `home`, `style`, `agents`) and the description is imperative and sentence-case, for example `bios: Use ui-sdk shortcuts for reboot`. Omit the prefix only for project-wide changes, such as `Bump Next.js to 16.4.6`. No trailing period and no Co-authored-by trailers.
- `main` is the only long-lived branch; there is no `develop`. Do every change on a short kebab-case branch off `main` named after its area, such as `bios-ui-sdk`, and rely on Vercel preview deployments to review it.
- Squash-merge pull requests into `main`, so the commit title ends with the PR number, for example `home: Add GitHub link (#4)`. The PR description uses the sections `Summary` (what, why, and `Closes #n`), `Testing` (how it was verified), `Self-review`, and a final `Release Notes:` line followed by one bullet: `Added …`, `Fixed …`, `Improved …`, or `N/A`.
- Do not add explanatory comments to committed code; document durable design decisions here instead.
- Community files live at the root (`LICENSE`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md`, `SECURITY.md`) and in `.github/` (`CODEOWNERS`, the pull request template). The MIT License covers code only; keep its personal-content exclusion when content moves.
- `.rules` is the single source of agent instructions; `AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md` are symlinks to it.
